ine-height:100%"&gt;3,222&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:1.5pt 1pt 1.5pt 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:1.5pt 0 1.5pt 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;3,028&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:1.5pt 1pt 1.5pt 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="6" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:1.5pt 1pt 1.5pt 1.02pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;Non-cash investing activities:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:1.5pt 1pt 1.5pt 1.02pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;Changes in accrued plant additions&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:1.5pt 0 1.5pt 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;(24,735)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:1.5pt 1pt 1.5pt 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:1.5pt 0 1.5pt 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;1,421&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:1.5pt 1pt 1.5pt 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;margin-top:30pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:139%"&gt;New Accounting Standards&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;margin-top:9pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:139%"&gt;New Accounting Standards Adopted&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;margin-top:9pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:22.5pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;In December 2023, the FASB issued ASU 2023-09, Income Taxes (Topic 740), Improvements to Income Tax Disclosures, to enhance income tax disclosures for the effective tax rate reconciliation and income taxes paid to address investor requests for more information about the tax risks and opportunities. This update applies to all reporting entities subject to Accounting Standards Codification 740: Income Tax. For non-public business entities, ASU 2023-09 requires qualitative disclosure about specific categories of reconciling items and individual jurisdictions that result in a significant difference between the statutory tax rate and the effective tax rate; disclosure of income taxes paid (net of refunds received), disaggregated by federal, state and foreign; and net amounts paid to an individual jurisdiction that are equal to or exceed 5% of the total income taxes paid (net of refunds received). In addition, ASU 2023-09 requires disclosure of income (or loss) from continuing operations before income tax expense (or benefit) disaggregated between domestic and foreign; and income tax expense (or benefit) from continuing operations disaggregated by federal, state, and foreign. ASU 2023-09 also provides guidance to eliminate certain disclosures related to unrecognized tax benefit balances. For non-public business entities, ASU 2023-09 is effective for annual periods beginning after December 15, 2025. ASU 2023-09 is applied on a prospective basis with the option to apply the standard retrospectively. EPE adopted ASU 2023-09 effective January 1, 2026, and the adoption did not materially impact EPE&#8217;s disclosures.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;margin-top:9pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:22.5pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;In July 2025, the FASB issued ASU 2025-05, Financial Instruments-Credit Losses (Topic 326), Measurement of Credit Losses for Accounts Receivable and Contract Assets, to address challenges encountered when applying the guidance in Topic 326, Financial Instruments-Credit Losses, to current accounts receivable and current contract assets arising from transactions accounted for under Topic 606, Revenue from Contracts with Customers. The amendments in this ASU provide (i) all entities with a practical expedient and (ii) entities other than public business entities with an accounting policy election when estimating expected credit losses for current accounts receivable and current contract assets arising from transactions accounted for under Topic 606, to consider collection activity after the balance sheet date when developing its estimate of expected credit losses. ASU 2025-05 will be effective for annual and interim reporting periods beginning after December 15, 2025. EPE adopted ASU 2025-05 on a prospective basis, effective January 1, 2026, and the adoption did not materially impact EPE&#8217;s financial statements.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:22.5pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;In August 2025, the FASB issued ASU 2025-06, Intangibles-Goodwill and Other-Internal-Use Software (Subtopic 350-40), Targeted Improvements to the Accounting for Internal-Use Software, to modernize the accounting for software costs that are accounted for under Subtopic 350-40, Intangibles-Goodwill and Other-Internal-Use Software (referred to as "internal-use software"). ASU 2025-06 removes all references to prescriptive and sequential software development states (referred to as "project states") throughout Subtopic 350-40. Furthermore, this ASU supersedes the website development costs guidance and incorporates the recognition requirements for website-specific development costs into the internal-use software standard, and &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;requires that disclosures for internal-use software costs align with those in Subtopic 360-10 for property, plant, and equipment. ASU 2025-06 applies to all entities subject to the internal-use software and website development costs guidance in Subtopic 350-40 and 350-50, respectively. Entities are permitted to apply the new guidance using (i) a prospective transition approach, (ii) a modified transition approach that is based on the status of the project and whether software costs were capitalized before the date of adoption and (iii) a retrospective transition approach. ASU 2025-06 will be effective for annual and interim reporting periods beginning after December 15, 2027 and early adoption is permitted. EPE adopted ASU 2025-06 effective January 1, 2026, and the adoption did not materially impact EPE&#8217;s financial statements.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:22.5pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;In December 2025, the FASB issued ASU 2025-10, Government Grants (Topic 832), to provide accounting guidance for business entities receiving government grants. ASU 2025&#8209;10 provides comprehensive recognition and disclosure guidance for monetary and tangible non-monetary grants, while specifically excluding income tax credits, below&#8209;market loans, guarantees, transfers of intangible assets, and grants to not&#8209;for&#8209;profit entities. ASU 2025-10 requires an entity to recognize a government grant when it becomes probable that the entity will both comply with the grant&#8217;s conditions and receive the associated benefits. ASU 2025-10 allows entities to elect to recognize the government grants (i) related to an asset either as deferred income or as an adjustment to the cost basis of the asset and (ii) related to income either as other income or as a reduction from the related expenses. ASU 2025&#8209;10 will be effective for annual and interim reporting periods beginning after December 15, 2029 for non-public entities, with early adoption permitted. EPE adopted ASU 2025-10 effective January 1, 2026, and the adoption did not materially impact EPE&#8217;s financial statements.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;margin-top:9pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:139%"&gt;New Accounting Standards to be Adopted in the Future&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify;text-indent:22.5pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;The new accounting standard discussed below is issued by the FASB and is to be applied to financial statements prepared in accordance with GAAP. Differences may occur between financial statements prepared in accordance with GAAP and financial statements prepared in accordance with the USoA when these standards are adopted.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;margin-top:9pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:22.5pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;In May 2026, the FASB issued ASU 2026-02, Environmental Credits and Environmental Credit Obligations (Topic 818), to improve the financial accounting for and disclosure of environmental credits and environmental credit obligations. This update applies to all entities and affects entities that acquire, generate, transfer, or hold environmental credits and entities with obligations arising from environmental regulatory compliance programs that may be settled using environmental credits. ASU 2026-02 establishes guidance for the recognition, measurement, presentation, and disclosure of environmental credits and environmental credit obligations. The amendments require environmental credits to be recognized and measured based on their intended use and method of acquisition, while environmental credit obligations are recognized and measured based on the nature of the obligation and the extent to which an entity expects to settle the obligation using environmental credits it holds. For non-public business entities, ASU 2026-02 is effective for annual periods beginning after December 15, 2028 and interim reporting periods within those annual reporting periods. Early adoption is permitted as of the beginning of an annual reporting period. ASU 2026-02 is applied on a retrospective basis through a cumulative-effect adjustment to the opening balance of retained earnings (or other appropriate components of equity or net assets) as of the beginning of the annual reporting period of adoption. EPE is currently assessing the impact of ASU 2026-02.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:14pt;text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;B.&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;Regulation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;margin-top:9pt;text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:139%"&gt;General&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;margin-top:2pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:22.5pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;The rates and services of EPE are regulated by incorporated municipalities in Texas, the Public Utility Commission of Texas ("PUCT"), the New Mexico Public Regulation Commission ("NMPRC") and the FERC. Municipal orders, ordinances and other agreements regarding rates and services adopted by Texas municipalities are subject to review and approval by the PUCT. The FERC has jurisdiction over EPE's wholesale (sales for resale) transactions, transmission service and compliance with federally-mandated reliability standards. The decisions of the PUCT, the NMPRC and the FERC are subject to judicial review. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify;text-indent:22.5pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;EPE&#8217;s regulatory matters discussed below are updates from December 31, 2025, through the filing of this report. For further discussion of EPE&#8217;s regulatory matters, see Note D of Notes to Financial Statements in the 2025 FERC Form No. 1.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;margin-top:9pt;text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:139%"&gt;Texas Regulatory Matters&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:22.5pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;2025 Texas Retail Rate Case Filing.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt; On January 27, 2025, EPE filed in Docket No. 57568, a request for an increase of approximately $129.0 million, or $85.7 million after accounting for the Distribution Cost Recovery Factor ("DCRF") and the Generation Cost Recovery Rider ("GCRR"), in non-fuel base revenues with the PUCT and those Texas municipalities that retained original jurisdiction. On April 2, 2026, the PUCT issued the PUCT Final Order in Docket No. 57568 ("2025 PUCT Final Order"), which provides among other things, for the following (i) an annual non-fuel base rate increase of $83.3 million; (ii) a return on equity of 9.4%; (iii) a 51%/49% equity to debt ratio; (iv) inclusion of 100% of EPE's capital additions in rate base; and (v) denial of EPE's request to allocate 100% of certain, specified generation resources to EPE's Texas jurisdiction including Newman Unit 6. The final rates approved in this case were effective May 1, 2026. EPE will assess a surcharge for the relate-back period for electricity consumed on and after July 1, 2025. EPE recorded a cumulative adjustment for relate-back revenues in the same reporting period that the final order was issued. On July 8, 2026, EPE filed an amended residential and relate-back tariff to be effective August 1, 2026. On July 20, 2026, PUCT Staff filed its recommendation that EPE&#8217;s tariffs be found sufficient and on July 29, 2026, the Administrative Law Judges ("ALJ") concurred and closed the docket.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:22.5pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;Fuel and Purchased Power Costs.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt; On February 13, 2026, EPE filed a request with the PUCT, which was assigned PUCT Docket No. 59373, to implement a Texas fixed fuel factor refund of $40.1 million over a three-month period beginning in March 2026. As of December 31, 2025, EPE had a net fuel over-recovery balance of approximately $39.6 million in Texas. PUCT Docket No. 59373 reflects accumulation of significant levels of margins on off-system sales, which are required to be shared with retail customers. On February 24, 2026, EPE's refund was approved by the ALJ on an interim basis effective for the first billing cycle of March 2026. On March 16, 2026, PUCT Staff filed a recommendation that notice be found sufficient and a recommendation for final disposition that the refund be approved. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:22.5pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;On July 31, 2026, EPE filed a request with the PUCT, which was assigned PUCT Docket No. 60071, to implement a Texas fixed fuel factor refund of $47.0 million over a three-month period beginning in August 2026. EPE cannot predict the outcome of this filing. As of June 30, 2026, EPE had a net fuel over-recovery balance of approximately $60.0 million in Texas.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:22.5pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;Fuel Reconciliation Proceeding&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;. On September 27, 2024, EPE filed an application with the PUCT, which was assigned PUCT Docket No. 57149, to reconcile $284.8 million of Texas fuel and purchased power expenses incurred during the period April 1, 2022, through March 31, 2024. The City of El Paso, Texas Industrial Energy Consumers, Freeport McMoran, the Office of Public Utility Council, and Vinton Steel have moved to intervene in the proceeding. EPE filed a list of issues on October 21, 2024. EPE presented a settlement proposal to the parties on April 4, 2025. On May 7, 2025, the parties responded to EPE's settlement proposal, and on May 8, 2025, the parties agreed to abate the proceeding to allow settlement discussions. On May 9, 2025, the State Office of Administrative Hearings issued an order abating the proceeding. With the issuance of 2025 PUCT Final Order, EPE and the parties have resumed settlement discussions. EPE cannot predict the outcome of this filing. The April 1, 2024 through June 30, 2026, Texas jurisdictional fuel and purchased power costs subject to a future prudence review by the PUCT total approximately $72.7 million.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:22.5pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;Generation Cost Recovery Rider.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt; In the PUCT Final Order in Docket No. 52195 ("2021 PUCT Final Order"), a GCRR baseline was adopted which would allow recovery of approximately 80% of the final capital costs of Newman Unit 6, representing approximately 228 Megawatt(s) ("MW") of the natural gas-fired generating unit, from Texas customers. On February 21, 2023, EPE filed an application with the PUCT, which was assigned PUCT Docket No. 54659, for approval of a GCRR designed to recover capital costs effective once Newman Unit 6 began commercial operation in 2023. Newman Unit 6 began commercial operation on December 27, 2023, and EPE began billing approved GCRR rates on February 1, 2024. The approved rates reflect 80% of Newman Unit 6 costs through December 31, 2022, and EPE had 60 days from the commercial operation date ("COD") to file updated rates which reflect full facility costs through the COD. On February 23, 2024, EPE filed an application with the PUCT, which was assigned PUCT Docket No. 56225, to update the approved GCRR rates to reflect the &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;full cost of Newman Unit 6 through COD to be recovered from its Texas customers. Interim rates reflecting the Texas jurisdictional portion of the full cost for Newman Unit 6 were approved by the PUCT and were effective in customer billings on May 1, 2024. On January 31, 2025, the PUCT adopted the Proposal for Decision approving EPE's application. With the issuance of 2025 PUCT Final Order, the cost of Newman Unit 6 is moved to rate base and reflected in revised rates. Effective May 1, 2026, the GCRR related to Newman Unit 6 is reset to zero.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:22.5pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;On April 16, 2025, EPE filed an application with the PUCT to amend its GCRR to include the Texas allocated share of its actual investment in Texas Solar One ("Felina") through December 31, 2024, which was assigned Docket No. 57974, allowing the recovery of an additional Texas GCRR revenue requirement of $13.4 million. Interim rates recovering the $13.4 million were approved by the ALJ and were effective in billing on October 1, 2025. An agreed upon paper hearing and briefing schedule concluded on November 21, 2025. On October 10, 2025, EPE filed an application to further amend its GCRR to include the recovery of an additional Texas GCRR revenue requirement of $3.6 million to reflect the full cost of Felina through its COD of August 29, 2025. This filing was assigned Docket No. 58834. On October 30, 2025, EPE filed a motion to consolidate both GCRR amendment proceedings. On November 3, 2025, EPE&#8217;s motion was granted requiring the consolidated proceeding to be handled in Docket No. 57974. Interim rates were adopted effective December 1, 2025. EPE circulated a settlement proposal, and on February 3, 2026, filed request for abatement of the consolidated proceeding which was granted on February 4, 2026. EPE cannot predict the outcome of these filings.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:22.5pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;Texas Business Solar Program ("TXBSP&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt; On June 26, 2023, EPE filed an application with the PUCT, which was assigned PUCT Docket No. 55176, for approval of a TXBSP designed for EPE&#8217;s mid to large commercial, industrial, educational, and non-military governmental customers. TXBSP would be supported by 50 MW of the 150 MW solar facility approved by the PUCT in Docket No. 54929. On September 23, 2024, the ALJ issued a proposed order approving an uncontested settlement. On January 16, 2025, the PUCT issued a Final Order approving EPE's application. The PUCT subsequently approved an application filed by EPE to modify the treatment of Renewable Energy Certificates ("RECs") received by participating customers, allowing registration of RECs with Western Renewable Energy Generation Information System, an independent tracking system for RECs. The change was approved on April 2, 2026. Generation credits provided to subscribers were also updated following issuance of the 2025 PUCT Final Order, effective May 1, 2026.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:22.5pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;Distribution Cost Recovery Factor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;. On June 26, 2026, EPE filed an application with the PUCT, which was assigned PUCT Docket No. 59936, for approval of a DCRF to recover distribution capital investments made from October 1, 2024 (the day after the end of the test year in EPE's last base rate case, Docket No. 57568) through March 31, 2026. Adjusted for load growth, the requested Texas retail DCRF revenue requirement is $37.6 million. EPE cannot predict the outcome of this filing.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:22.5pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;Purchase Power Capacity Cost Recovery Factor ("PCRF&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;"). On June 15, 2026, EPE filed an application with the PUCT, which was assigned PUCT Docket No. 59889, for approval of a PCRF for recovery of $14.4 million in jurisdictional purchased power capacity costs associated with the battery energy storage system component of the Milagro facility and the imputed capacity of both the Buena Vista and Milagro solar facilities that are not currently recovered in EPE&#8217;s base rates. EPE cannot predict the outcome of this filing. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:22.5pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;Other Required Approvals&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;. EPE has obtained other required approvals for tariffs and other approvals required by the Texas Public Utility Regulatory Act and the PUCT.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;margin-top:9pt;text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:139%"&gt;New Mexico Regulatory Matters&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:22.5pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;2026 New Mexico Rate Case Filing.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt; On March 27, 2026, EPE filed its 2026 Application for Revision of Retail Electric Rates, requesting a base revenue requirement increase of $70.4 million. The application was assigned NMPRC Case No. 25-00082-UT. A prehearing conference was held on April 15, 2026. On July 7, 2026, the Hearing Examiner issued an order vacating the current procedural schedule and requiring EPE to amend its application by August 6, 2026. EPE filed an amended application on August 6, 2026. EPE cannot predict the outcome of this proceeding.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:22.5pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;Fuel and Purchased Power Costs. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;On February 20, 2025, the NMPRC issued a Final Order Adopting Recommended Decisions with Modifications approving EPE&#8217;s amended application and requiring EPE to address the New Mexico Fuel and Purchased Power Cost Adjustment Clause ("FPPCAC") 12-month rolling methodology in EPE&#8217;s next general rate case and conduct a limited audit on FPPCAC calculation and billing. On December 12, 2025, EPE filed a Notice of Filing of Report of Independent Accountants on FPPCAC calculation and billing. As of June 30, 2026, EPE had a net fuel over-recovery balance of approximately $8.2 million related to the FPPCAC in New Mexico. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:22.5pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;Afton to Airport Certificate of Convenience and Necessity (&#8220;CCN&#8221;). &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;On July 10, 2026, in Docket No. 26-0000137 EPE filed an application for a CCN to construct, own and operate a new 345 kilovolt transmission line between EPE&#8217;s Afton North and Airport substations and for right of way width approval. A public hearing is scheduled to begin November 5, 2026. The estimated cost of the project is $43.2 million. EPE cannot predict the outcome of this proceeding.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:22.5pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;Eddy County Converter Station (&#8220;Eddy Tie&#8221;) CCN. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;On April 13, 2026, in Docket No. 26-0000071, EPE filed an application for a CCN for a replacement of the existing 200 MW High Voltage Direct Current ("HVDC ") Eddy Tie facility with a new 200 MW HVDC. The estimated cost of the project is $289.0 million.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;A public hearing is scheduled for August 18, 2026. EPE cannot predict the outcome of this proceeding.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:22.5pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;Long-Term Debt and Revolving Credit Facility &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;("RCF")&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;Under the authorization in Case No. 25-00018-UT, on May 12, 2026, EPE issued and funded 5.58% and 6.29% Senior Unsecured Notes with aggregate principal amounts of $80.0 million and $220.0 million, respectively, with maturity dates of May 12, 2036 and May 12, 2056, respectively. On July 2, 2026, in Case No. 26-0000135, EPE filed a securities application to amend and increase its maximum borrowing limit under its RCF from $550.0 million to $650.0 million and to issue up to $1.2 billion of fixed rate long-term debt. The application was not opposed, the Hearing Examiner vacated the public hearing set for August 4, 2026, and issued a Recommended Decision approving EPE&#8217;s application and final disposition is expected by August 13, 2026. EPE cannot predict the outcome of this proceeding.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:22.5pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;New Mexico Renewable Energy Act Plan ("REA")&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;. On May 1, 2026, in Case No. 26-000093, EPE filed its Renewable Energy Act Plan for 2027 seeking approval to include the remaining 100 MW of the storage component of the Santa Teresa Project into its New Mexico REA portfolio at an estimated cost of $15.5 million. A public hearing was held on July 27 and July 28, 2026. EPE cannot predict the outcome of this proceeding.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:22.5pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;Other Required Approvals.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt; EPE has obtained other required approvals for tariffs and other approvals as required by the New Mexico Public Utility Act and the NMPRC.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;margin-top:9pt;text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:139%"&gt;Federal Regulatory Matters&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;margin-top:9pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:22.5pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;Long-Term Debt and Revolving Credit Facility. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;Under the authorization in Docket No. ES25-32-000, on May 12, 2026, EPE issued and funded 5.58% and 6.29% Senior Unsecured Notes with aggregate principal amounts of $80.0 million and $220.0 million, respectively, with maturity dates of May 12, 2036 and May 12, 2056, respectively. On June 23, 2026, in Docket No. ES26-54-000, EPE filed an Application for Authorization with the FERC under Section 204 of the Federal Power Act, seeking approval to: (i) issue new long-term securities in an amount not to exceed $1.2 billion; (ii) raise the maximum borrowing amount of its RCF from $550.0 million to $650.0 million; and (iii) refinance certain of its existing pollution control bonds. EPE requested that the FERC issue an order on or before August 24, 2026.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:22.5pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;Other Required Approvals.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt; EPE has obtained required approvals for rates, tariffs and other approvals as required by the Federal Power Act and the FERC.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;margin-top:14pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;C.&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;Income Taxes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:22.5pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;EPE files its income tax returns as a consolidated group in the U.S. federal jurisdiction and in the states of Texas, New&#160;Mexico and Arizona. EPE's financial statements are presented on EPE's stand-alone basis. As of June 30, 2026, EPE does &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;not have any tax payments due to affiliates. EPE is no longer subject to tax examination by the taxing authorities in all jurisdictions in which EPE files income tax returns for years prior to 2021.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:22.5pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;For the three months ended June 30, 2026 and 2025, EPE&#8217;s regulatory-basis effective tax rate was 12.9% and 16.9%, respectively. For the six months ended June 30, 2026 and 2025, EPE&#8217;s regulatory-basis effective tax rate was 12.0% and 16.0%, respectively. EPE&#8217;s regulatory-basis effective tax rate for all periods differs from the federal statutory tax rate due to state income taxes and the amortization of excess deferred income taxes. In 2026, the effective tax rate was further impacted by production tax credits which were not recognized in 2025, and other permanent differences.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:139%"&gt;D.&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;Commitments, Contingencies and Uncertainties&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:139%"&gt;Litigation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:22.5pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;EPE is involved in various legal, environmental, tax and regulatory proceedings before various courts, regulatory commissions and governmental agencies regarding matters arising in the ordinary course of business. In many of these matters, EPE has excess casualty liability insurance that covers the various claims, actions and complaints. EPE regularly analyzes current information and, as necessary, makes provisions in its regulatory-basis financial statements for probable liabilities for the eventual disposition of these matters. While the outcome of these matters cannot be predicted with certainty, based upon a review of the matters and applicable insurance coverage, EPE believes that none of these matters will have a material adverse effect on the financial position, results of operations or cash flows of EPE. EPE typically expenses legal costs, including expenses related to loss contingencies, as they are incurred.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:139%"&gt;Power Purchase and Sale Contracts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:22.5pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;To supplement its own generation and operating reserve requirements and to meet its Renewable Portfolio Standard ("RPS") requirements, EPE engages in power purchase arrangements that may vary in duration and amount based on an evaluation of EPE&#8217;s resource needs, the economics of the transactions and specific RPS requirements. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:22.5pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;On February 3, 2026, EPE entered into two 20-year power purchase agreements with Desert Pine Solar, LLC and Desert Pine Storage, LLC to purchase up to 150 MW plus up to 150 MW of energy storage, respectively, at the generating and energy storage facility located in El Paso County, Texas, which has an anticipated COD in May 2028.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:22.5pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;On February 26, 2026, EPE entered into an exchange agreement with Dynasty Power Inc., whereby EPE delivers up to 75 MW at Palo Verde and receives an equal amount at the Palo Verde delivery point from March 1, 2026 to December 31, 2026.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:22.5pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt; See Note I of Notes to Financial Statements in the 2025 FERC Form No. 1 for further discussion of the significant agreements EPE has entered into with various counterparties for the purchase and sale of electricity.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:139%"&gt;Significant Customers &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:22.5pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;On March 18, 2026, EPE executed an agreement with Wurldwide LLC to provide electrical services to its planned data center facilities in the region, which are expected to grow to 1 gigawatt ("GW") of capacity in the ensuing years. Revenue impact for 2026 is expected to be limited, as load during 2026&#8211;2027 will likely be associated with commissioning and partial energization activities and is anticipated to represent only a limited portion of the project&#8217;s ultimate 1 GW capacity, with more occurring as data center facilities are placed into service in subsequent periods.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:139%"&gt;Environmental Matters&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:22.5pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;EPE is subject to extensive laws, regulations and permit requirements with respect to air and greenhouse gas emissions, water discharges, soil and water quality, waste management and disposal, natural resources and other environmental matters by federal, state, regional, tribal and local authorities. Failure to comply with such laws, regulations and requirements can result in actions by authorities or other third parties that might seek to impose on EPE administrative, civil and/or criminal penalties or other sanctions. In addition, releases of pollutants or contaminants into the environment can result in costly cleanup liabilities. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;These laws, regulations and requirements are subject to change through modification or reinterpretation, or the introduction of new laws and regulations and, as a result, EPE may face additional capital and operating costs to comply. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify;text-indent:22.5pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;National Ambient Air Quality Standards ("NAAQS")&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;. Under the U.S. Clean Air Act ("CAA"), the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency ("EPA") sets NAAQS for six criteria pollutants considered harmful to public health and the environment, including particulate matter ("PM&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:6.47pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%;position:relative;top:1.4pt;vertical-align:baseline"&gt;2.5&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;"), nitrogen oxide, carbon monoxide, ozone, sulfur dioxide, and lead.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify;text-indent:22.5pt"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:22.5pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;On June 26, 2026, the District of Columbia Circuit Court of Appeals upheld the 2024 EPA rule resetting the PM&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:6.47pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%;position:relative;top:1.4pt;vertical-align:baseline"&gt;2.5 &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;NAAQS from 12.0 &#181;g/m&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:6.47pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%;position:relative;top:-3.48pt;vertical-align:baseline"&gt;3&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt; to 9.0 &#181;g/m&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:6.47pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%;position:relative;top:-3.48pt;vertical-align:baseline"&gt;3&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;. Following this decision, the court established a deadline of February 6, 2027 for the EPA to finalize nonattainment designations. EPE will continue to monitor any consequences of the rule on the region&#8217;s attainment status.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:22.5pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;Environmental Regulation. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;On February 19, 2026, the Texas Commission on Environmental Quality adopted proposed amendments to the Texas Administrative Code Title 30, Chapter 39 (Public Notice) and Chapter 55 (Requests for Reconsideration and Contested Case Hearings; Public Comment) that went into effect on May 1, 2026. The amendments increase public visibility, scrutiny, and hearing risk for air permit actions, including new generation unit construction and major modifications. Increased public scrutiny can potentially have an impact on construction and major modifications projects schedule, cost, and legal risk if not proactively managed. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:22.5pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;See Note I of Notes to Financial Statements in 2025 FERC Form No. 1 for further discussion. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:139%"&gt;E. &#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;Financial Instruments and Investments&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:22.5pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;The FASB guidance requires EPE to disclose estimated fair values for its financial instruments. EPE has determined that cash and temporary investments, investment in debt securities, accounts receivable, decommissioning trust funds ("NDT"), that are reflected in Other Special Funds in the Regulatory-Basis Balance Sheet, long-term debt, financing obligations and capital lease obligations, short-term borrowings under the term loan, the RCF, accounts payable and customer deposits meet the definition of financial instruments. The carrying amounts of cash and temporary investments, accounts receivable, accounts payable, customer deposits, and the term loan approximate fair value because of the short maturity of these items. Investments in debt securities and NDT are carried at estimated fair value.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:22.5pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;Long-Term Debt, Financing Obligations, Capital Lease Obligations and Short-Term Borrowings Under the Term Loan and the RCF.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt; The fair values of EPE's long-term debt, financing obligations, capital lease obligations including the current portion thereof, and short-term borrowings under the term loan and the RCF are based on estimated market prices for similar issues and are presented below (in thousands):&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:5pt;text-align:center"&gt;&lt;table style="border-collapse:collapse;display:inline-table;margin-bottom:5pt;vertical-align:text-bottom;width:99.699%"&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:49.779%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:10.630%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.533%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:10.630%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.533%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:10.630%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.533%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:10.632%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:1.5pt 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="9" style="padding:1.5pt 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:center"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:8pt;font-weight:700;line-height:100%"&gt;June 30,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="9" style="padding:1.5pt 1pt;text-align:center;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:8pt;font-weight:700;line-height:100%"&gt;December 31,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:1.5pt 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="9" style="padding:1.5pt 1pt;text-align:center;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:8pt;font-weight:700;line-height:100%"&gt;2026&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="9" style="padding: